Z||000011||shiloh187||04-17-2001||08:22 PM||||the module? What should I get to try and get this to work? The way he said he knows it is not the starter is because he took it out, cleaned it up,  and hooked it up to another system and it turned it over..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000012||LZeitgeist||04-17-2001||08:43 PM||||If you plan on push-starting it, you need to bypass the cutoff switch on the clutch pedal...||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||04-17-2001||08:48 PM||||Omg I just realized what you said It just cicks but does not turn over? and you had tested the starter and found it to be ok? hummmm is the engine siezed???????<P><BR>IF the engine cranks over but does not start<P>1 check that your getting Fuel whip off the top of the air cleaner and crank it a few times? smell fuel? YES? ok then Pull off the coil wire from the distributer cap and stick a spark plug in it rest the spark plug against the engine somewhere away from a fuel source (PREFERED METHOD) have a friend crank the engine.. See sparks? NO!? Ok we have narrowed it down to a spark problem  Go out and get yourself a Haynes manual and an Ohm meter (DIGITAL!) yeah yeah it costs a bit more but its worth it. Now turn to pages 149 thru 151 there you will se how to use that fancy digital meter to test your coil and pickup coil. If theese parts are good then reach into you pocket for about 60 bucks and get an ignition module. trust me getting a meter and a manual will save you big bucks in the long run<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||04-17-2001||09:15 PM||||What was the 'other system' that the starter was checked with?<BR>Any idea how many 'AMPS' it is pulling?<BR>If the starter doesn't go wirrrr.... and the belts won't go turn, turn, turn . . . . How could it be the module?<BR>If you push it, you don't need to worry about the clutch pedal switch!||||reg||1
