Z||000017||shiloh187||04-18-2001||03:11 AM||||**update**<P>i got it back from the guy tonight.  after checking all the wires connected to the starter, battery, and the such he came across a frayed wire (the people I bought this from really did a number on this car)...<P>he replaced the wire and it fixed seemingly all the problems we were having, cept a small oil leak that we cant figure out where it is coming from.  but it turned right over, and is idling by itself now.<P>only thing i have to replace now are relays on the thermostat? to tell the fan when to kick on cause it dont on its own right now.<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000018||Indiana_resto_guy||04-18-2001||09:36 AM||||OK, then. You must have lost something in your description(s).<BR>The fan switch circut operates with a signal from the temp. switch on the engine that energizes the relay. An easy way to check if you have A/C is to turn it on. If the fan comes on, the relay is good. If it does not, hot wire the fan to make sure it is good. Next locate the temp switch on the engine (4 cylinder back side next to tranny, green wires) and jumper them together, fan should come on. If it does, replace the switch, if it doesen't you have another bad wire someplace.<BR>(V-6 switch is below the coil, green wires)||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phil||04-18-2001||10:44 AM||||Correction --the fan switch on a V6 is in the head over by  and below the thermostat housing (1 wire). The sensor by the dist. is for the gage and idiot light.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Indiana_resto_guy||04-18-2001||01:32 PM||||OOPS!<BR>My fault, Thanks Phil||||reg||1
Z||000021||shiloh187||04-19-2001||10:08 PM||||okay so we had it back for two days now, and we had a small oil leak still but could not figure out from where.  Although, it was burning it up pretty nice.  everytime we drive, the oil would just smoke alot, straight out the trunk.<P>but now, we are driving home in it, and we slow down, it stalls.  it will not click, crank over, anything at all.  no sounds, nothing.  the bateery is still powering the lights, radio, etc.  so we push it really fast, like 5 mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and pop the clutch and it starts up and drives, until we slow down again.  damn!!!<P>shiloh||||reg||9
Z||000022||shiloh187||04-20-2001||03:41 AM||||i was reading this post:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009410.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009410.html</A> <P>and noticed he had white smoke pouring out his decklids as do i.  I figured I would bring it back up to see about any ideas.  We have just replaced the head gaskets and had the head milled to ensure proper fitting.<P>ideas? remember i know nothing about cars, but am learning now.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000023||Taijiguy||04-20-2001||06:59 AM||||If you can't locate the leak on your own, instead of "Easter Egging" the problem, save your money, and take it to a shop that can do ultraviolet leak detection. Degrease the motor as best as you can, or steam clean it, then take it straight down to the shop where they'll put a dye in the oil. They'll tell you to drive it for a day or two, and bring it back. Then they can shine their black light on the motor and identify the leak location. This only works if it's actually a leak. Make sure you aren't actually burning the oil, in which case, short of a rebuild, there is no real fix.<BR>Good luck,<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000024||shiloh187||04-20-2001||01:15 PM||||TaijiGuy:<P>if i was burning oil, would it be pouring out the decklids of the exhaust?<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000025||84Bill||04-20-2001||01:19 PM||||If it were leaking out of the rocker cover most definatly!||||reg||1
